/* -------------------------------------------------------------------- */
** -------							------- **
** -------	How to use the SignalHandler Object ...		------- **
** -------							------- **
** -------------------------------------------------------------------- */


Description : 	This object allows the application to handle signals
	      	(as SIGSEGV, SIGPIPE, ...) and to associate one or more
		callback to this events.
		So when the event occurs, callbacks are called in order
		of creation.
		You can change function and/or data of a callback.
		You can delete a callback for one signal or all the
		callbacks of the signal.

Create the Object : 	NEW()
			No parameter.
			return value : the object, NULL if an error occured.

			This function creates the SignalHandler used in the
			application. You can create ONLY ONE handler at a
			time. (why more than one ???)
			If an error occurs (memory allocation error, ...)
			NULL pointer will be returned.


Delete The Object :	DELETE(*SignalHandler)
			Parameter : Pointer to SignalHandler;
			return value : none


			This function deletes the SignalHandler.
			All defaults handlers are set before the function
			returns.


Copy this object :	Not implemented (not usefull : there should be only
			ONE SignalHandler)


Add a Callback :	Add_Callback(Signal, Function, Data)
			Parameters : 	int 	Signal
					void	(*Function)()
					void	*Data
			return value : int

			This function adds a Callback for the signal 'Signal'.
			This function should return void.
			When 'Signal' occurs, 'Function' is called and its
			first parameter is 'Data'.
			More than one callback doesn't matter : there called
			by creation order.


Remove a Callback :	Remove_Callback(Signal, Function)
			Parameters : 	int 	Signal
					void	(*Function)()
			return value : int;

			This function deletes callback(s) for a signal.
			If 'Signal' has no callback, no error is returned.
			if 'Function' equals NULL then all callbacks of
			'Signal' will be removed, elses only the callback
			matching with 'Function' will be removed.

			return value : 	0 if succesfull.
					-1 if error (free error, ...)



Modify a callback : 	Modify_Callback(Signal, Function, NewFunction, NewData)
			Parameters : 	int	Signal
					void	(*Function)()
					void	(*NewFunction)()
					void	*NewData
			return value :  int

			This function modify the Callback 'Function' of the
			signal 'Signal'.
			If 'NewFunction' != -1 then the new callback function
			is NewFunction.
			if 'NewData' != -1 then the new data is 'NewData'

			Note that if 'Function' is used for an other signal,
			its value won't be change. This change operates only
			for the 'Signal' signal.

			return value : 	0 if succesfull
					-1 if an error ocurred